          "While React's SyntheticEvent system excels at managing discrete user interactions such as button clicks, form submissions, and checkbox toggles, it introduces severe architectural challenges when applied to high-frequency streaming events such as scroll, touchmove, and wheel. In high-performance web applications, these interactions require maintaining a rock-solid 60 frames per second\u2014or 120 frames per second on high-refresh ProMotion displays\u2014meaning every single animation and layout frame must complete within a strict budget of 16.6 milliseconds or 8.3 milliseconds.",
          "The core bottleneck arises from browser rendering architecture and thread scheduling. Modern browser engines split rendering across two distinct threads: the JavaScript Main Thread (which executes user scripts, handles DOM manipulation, and runs React's reconciliation engine) and the Compositor Thread (which handles scrolling, GPU layer compositing, and visual updates). When a user touches the screen or scrolls the mouse wheel, the browser compositor thread wants to shift the page pixels immediately on the GPU without waiting for JavaScript. However, the W3C event specification allows event handlers to call event.preventDefault() to cancel scrolling.",
