          "Just as object literals introduce reference instability, function declarations inside component bodies represent a major source of erratic effect behavior. In JavaScript, functions are first-class objects allocated on the heap. Consider a common architectural pattern: an engineer writes a helper function performSearch() in the component body, and then calls it inside useEffect: const performSearch = () => { api.query(searchTerm); }; useEffect(() => { performSearch(); }, [performSearch]);. Because performSearch is re-instantiated on every single render pass, its memory reference changes continuously. If performSearch updates state upon completion, the component enters an immediate, catastrophic infinite render loop.",
          "When confronted with function dependencies, junior developers frequently resort to removing the function from the dependency array, thereby triggering the exhaustive-deps lint warning and introducing stale closure bugs where performSearch queries with an outdated searchTerm. Senior systems architects resolve function dependency volatility using two primary methodologies: Closure Inlining and useCallback Stabilization.",
          "The primary and most elegant technique is Closure Inlining: if a function is used exclusively within a single useEffect hook, move the entire function definition inside the effect callback! By defining 'function performSearch() { ... }' inside useEffect, performSearch ceases to be an external dependency of the effect. The effect now depends solely on the primitive variables read by performSearch (such as searchTerm), completely eliminating function reference instability without allocating any caching hooks or consuming additional Fiber memory.",
