          "It is vital to recognize what useCallback actually does: evaluating const performSearch = useCallback(() => { ... }, [searchTerm]) still creates an ephemeral inline arrow function in JavaScript memory on every render pass! What useCallback prevents is not function creation, but reference churn down the dependency tree. Why doesn't React automatically deep-compare dependencies instead? Because deep-comparing arbitrary function scopes and nested object graphs is an O(N) recursive traversal that can easily exceed the 16.6-millisecond frame budget on complex state structures, whereas shallow Object.is comparison executes in microseconds.",
          "Therefore, senior engineers avoid applying useCallback indiscriminately across every function: caching simple inline event handlers passed to standard DOM elements (<button onClick={handleClick}>) wastes CPU cycles on dependency diffing without delivering any performance gains. useCallback should be reserved strictly for functions passed into effect dependencies or memoized component trees, preventing cascading reconciliations. In addition, when designing library APIs or design system components, returning stabilized callback references protects downstream consumers from accidental infinite loops and excessive reconciliation passes.",
          "Another critical nuance is the interaction between useCallback and custom hook abstractions. When writing custom hooks that expose action dispatchers or event handlers, wrapping those exposed functions in useCallback ensures that consuming components can safely list them in their own useEffect dependencies without fear of spurious re-runs. This architectural discipline forms the backbone of clean, composable hook ecosystems in enterprise codebases.",
